{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-916f7d86ba2663ff","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The workspace already has git initialized (the initial commit \"chore: initial workspace setup\" exists). The branch is `iotahub/phase-1`. The story states \"complete `git init` at the repository root and commit the scaffold files as the initial commit\" — since git has already been initialized, the story's work is mainly about the monorepo scaffold.\n\nTo understand the existing state, let me check the current state a bit more:\n- Is there a package.json at the root? It's not in the ls output. So it doesn't exist.\n- Is there an apps/ directory? Doesn't exist.\n- turbo.json? Doesn't exist.\n- README.md, .editorconfig? Don't exist.\n\nSo the work packages are as follows:\n1. Root package.json (name nextmove, private true, workspaces is apps/*, packageManager is npm@10, engines.node is 22.x, devDeps pinning turbo 2.x, scripts build/test/lint → turbo run ...)\n2. turbo.json with tasks build/test/lint (correct dependsOn + output caching for dist)\n3. apps/ directory (at least one app so that workspaces can resolve — hmm, npm workspaces with no matching packages is fine, but turbo run will probably say \"no packages found\"). Actually, for `npm install` to work and `npx turbo --version` to report 2.x, we just need turbo installed as a devDep. But `turbo run build` with no packages will warn \"No packages found in workspace\" and exit 0 (in turbo 2.x it exits 0 even with no packages — I think turbo 2.0 emits a warning but returns exit 0). Let me think... turbo 2.x: if no packages are found in the workspace, `turbo run build` exits 0 with a warning. Yes, I think turbo 2 exits 0 when there are no tasks. Actually, let me verify: turbo 1.x warned \"No packages found in workspace\" and exited 0. I think turbo 2 is the same.\n\nBut the acceptance criteria state: \"running `npm install` at the root completes with exit code 0, and `npx turbo --version` reports a 2.x version.\" So what's needed is just that npm install works and turbo is 2.x.\n\nAlso TDD/test strategy gate: \"pure configuration, scaffold, environment only (no runtime logic) | only sample passing unit tests. E2E is not required.\" So we need sample passing unit tests. Hmm — but where? At the root? This story is pure scaffolding. The gate says \"only sample passing unit tests. E2E is not required.\" So we should include a minimal test setup — a sample unit test that passes.\n\nWhere do the tests go? Options:\n- Create a minimal `apps/web` package? The story says workspaces array includes apps/*, but doesn't require apps to exist. However, a monorepo with an empty apps directory... let me think about what makes sense. The acceptance criteria only mention the root files. But `turbo run test` needs a package with a test script for something to run. With no packages, `turbo run test` exits 0 with a warning — that's acceptable but \"sample passing unit tests\" suggests actually running tests.\n\nSimplest and most robust approach: create `apps/web` as a minimal workspace package (a placeholder Next.js app is a later story; for now, a minimal package with a test script and a sample unit test). Hmm, but the story says \"implement only this story\" — later stories will probably create actual apps. Creating a minimal `apps/web` scaffold package with a sample test is reasonable and satisfies both \"workspaces array includes apps/*\" (npm works even with empty glob) and \"sample passing unit tests.\"\n\nActually, wait — let me re-read: \"the package manager is npm on Node 22.x, Turborepo major is 2.x. These defaults are declared here so that no later story will reinvent them.\" So this story is purely about the root scaffold. The test gate requires sample passing unit tests.\n\nLet me decide on the work packages:\n- WP1 (plan): Planner agent generates the plan.\n- WP2 (implementation):\n  - Create root `package.json` with the exact fields.\n  - Create `turbo.json` with build/test/lint tasks.\n  - Create `.editorconfig`, `README.md` (.gitignore already exists — verify it covers node_modules).\n  - Create `apps/` workspace: minimal `apps/web` placeholder? Or an empty directory?\n\nHmm, tricky point: npm workspaces with `\"workspaces\": [\"apps/*\"]` when there are no packages under apps/ — `npm install` works fine. But turbo 2 with no packages: `turbo run build` — I think it exits 0 with the warning \"No packages found in workspace.\" That's acceptable for the \"turbo run build\" script to work.\n\nBut for \"sample passing unit tests\": we can add a sample test at the root. For example, a root `test/` or a co-located test using node:test (Node 22 has a built-in test runner — no dependencies needed!). This is elegant: `node --test` with a sample test asserting that the scaffold files exist and that package.json/turbo.json have the required fields. That's actually a really good test for this story — unit tests verifying the scaffold itself (a meta-test). This maps 1:1 with acceptance criteria:\n\n- Test: root package.json exists, name is nextmove, private is true, workspaces is apps/*, packageManager is npm@10, engines.node is 22.x\n- Test: turbo 2.x is in devDependencies, scripts define build/test/lint as turbo run ...\n- Test: turbo.json has build/test/lint tasks with correct dependsOn and dist caching\n- Test: .gitignore, README.md, .editorconfig exist\n- Test: npx turbo --version reports 2.x (can exec this)\n\nUsing Node's built-in test runner (`node --test`), we avoid adding dependencies like jest/vitest. The root package.json's \"test\" script: `node --test tests/` — wait, but turbo.json's test task runs `turbo run test`, which runs `test` scripts of each package. The root itself is not a workspace member. So the sample unit test should be placed at the root and run directly via `npm test`, but `turbo run test` won't run it...\n\nHmm. Options:\na) Put the sample test in a minimal app package under apps/ (e.