st; npm install needs network. Let me verify network availability. The acceptance criterion requires `npm install` to exit 0, so network must be available (or there's a local registry/verdaccio). Let me check npm config and whether there's an offline cache.\n\nPlan of action as orchestrator:\n1. Do a light check of environment (node/npm versions, network) — I can run these read-only commands myself (orchestrator coordination; running checks is fine — not writing source code).\n2. Read story list summaries for coordination (jq ids/titles only).\n3. Kick off the planner sub-agent (read the ECC planner agent file → pass its full content as the prompt + task).\n4. Wait for the planner's result.\n5. Kick off the implementation sub-agent (which agent type? The loop contract says \"implementation sub-agent (spawned — never inline).\" There's no dedicated \"implementer\" agent file listed other than general ones. The loop contract table: plan → planner/architect; code → \"implementation sub-agent (spawned — never inline)\"; review → code-reviewer/security-reviewer; fix → build-error-resolver, etc. The spawn instructions say to pass the entire agent file content as the prompt for ECC agents. For implementation, I think we use a generic sub-agent (subagent_type: \"claude\" or \"general-purpose\") — the contract says don't set subagent_type unless instructed. Hmm: \"Never set model fields, and don't set subagent_type / run_in_background / name unless instructed below.\" So spawn with just prompt + description. The prompt should include the tdd-workflow skill instructions (\"the implementation sub-agent must load `tdd-workflow` before coding\"). I should tell the implementer to read .claude/skills/tdd-workflow/SKILL.md first.\n\nLet me check what skills exist in .claude/skills/.\n\n6. Wait for implementation, read the result.\n7. Kick off the code-reviewer sub-agent (pass the full content of ecc-code-reviewer.md + task).\n8. If failures → fix sub-agent (build-error-resolver).\n