ource code). But the implementation sub-agent should commit the scaffold as the \"initial commit\" of the scaffold files... wait, git is already initialized with an initial commit. The criterion \"complete git init at the repository root with exit code 0, and commit the scaffold files as the initial commit\" — since git init has already happened, the sub-agent should `git init` (idempotent, exit 0) and commit the scaffold files.\n\nHmm, who does the commit? Let me delegate: the implementation agent creates files and runs tests; then the marker procedure (Phase 5) is mine: touch marker, verify, `git add . && git commit`, push. The scaffold files themselves — should they be committed in a separate commit before the marker commit? The Phase 5 procedure says to commit with marker message \"chore: mark STORY-setup-001 as passed\" — that commit will include everything (git add .). So one commit containing scaffold + marker. This matches the literal instructions. But the criterion says \"commit the scaffold files as the initial commit\" — git is already initialized, so that part is satisfied by the existing repo; scaffold files get committed in the story commit. I think that's fine. Actually, to be cleaner, I could have the implementation agent commit the scaffold as a \"feat: scaffold nextmove monorepo\" commit, and then my Phase 5 commit adds the marker. Two commits. That better matches \"commit scaffold files as the initial commit.\" Let me instruct the implementation agent to commit the scaffold files after tests pass. Then Phase 5's commit adds the marker file. Both are good.\n\nWait, one more consideration about `git add .`: it will include .ralph/, ralph.log, .last-branch. ralph.log is a log — committing it to the repo is weird, but the harness's own instruction says `git add .`. Previous commit \"chore: initial workspace setup\" — let me check what's in the repo: `git ls-files`. If the harness intended logs to be committed, they'd be in .gitignore... .gitignore is 78 bytes; let me look. I'll check.\n\nAlso: is there a remote origin? \"Push commit to remote origin if connected: `git push origin HEAD 2>/dev/null || true`.\" git status says \"Your branch is up to date with 'origin/iotahub/phase-1'\" so origin exists.\n\nOK, environment checks to run now (read-only, orchestrator coordination):\n- node --version, npm --version\n- npm config get registry (check for local registry)\n- git ls-files (what's tracked)\n- cat .gitignore (78 bytes — small so OK to read)\n- ls .claude/skills/ and .claude/agents/\n- jq story id/title summary from prd.json\n\nLet me run these in parallel. Then kick off the planner.\n\nActually, one more double-check: does the environment have network for npm install? Let me do a quick `npm ping` with a short timeout. If offline, the implementer will need a workaround... let me check.\n\nNow, about the planner: the loop contract requires \"spawn and run planner + code-reviewer in every loop.\" So even for low-effort scaffolding, I must spawn the planner. I'll spawn it with the full content of ecc-planner.md + the task, have it generate an implementation plan for this story, and return the plan as its final message. The planner agent file's tools are Read/Grep/Glob — read-only, no issue. Note: the planner agent file has a `model: opus` frontmatter, but per the model rule, I never set the model in the spawn call — the Agent tool with no subagent_type... hmm, how do I make the Agent tool use a custom prompt as the system prompt? The spawn instructions say: `Agent(prompt=\"<ENTIRE content of the agent file + the task>\", description=\"<agent name>: <task>\")` and \"don't set subagent_type unless instructed.\" So just a plain Agent call with the entire agent file content + task in the prompt. The default subagent_type is general-purpose (or claude). Fine.\n\nThe planner runs asynchronously by default; I'll be notified. I can also use TaskOutput with block:true to wait. Per instructions: \"The Agent tool launches s