{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9b7e9a32fcf9894e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me check the skills more carefully. The skill directory contains `ecc`, `gitnexus`, `ui-ux-pro-max`. The tdd-workflow skill is probably inside the `ecc` directory. Let me check.\n\nAlso, an important point: the story list shows sub-stories: STORY-setup-001a (root package.json), 001b (turbo.json), 001c (README), 001d (.editorconfig) — these are child stories of the current story STORY-setup-001 (the parent story covers all of them). The current story I must complete is STORY-setup-001, and its acceptance criteria cover all of these. Good — I implement the parent story, which encompasses all of these items.\n\nSubsequent stories: apps/api (Express TypeScript API under Apps Api), apps/web (Angular 17 app), Prisma, etc. So the workspace structure is apps/api and apps/web. In this story, I only do the root scaffold. Should I create placeholder apps/api and apps/web packages? A subsequent story will scaffold them. For this story, `workspaces: [\"apps/*\"]` just needs to exist in package.json. To get `turbo run test` to run the sample unit tests, at least one workspace package with a test script is needed.\n\nHmm — should I create placeholder apps? Risk: STORY-setup-002 \"Scaffold Express TypeScript API Under Apps Api\" will create apps/api itself. If I create a placeholder apps/api/package.json, it might conflict with that story. A safer approach: create a neutral placeholder package like `apps/placeholder`? That's ugly, and subsequent stories might leave it behind. Or, run the sample unit test at the root without a workspace package… but then `turbo run test` runs nothing (turbo exits 0 with a \"No packages found\" warning).\n\nLet me think about what \"a sample passing unit test\" means for the test gate: \"pure configuration, scaffold, or environment-only (no runtime logic) | only a sample passing unit test; E2E not needed\".