{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-bc873bbccf27106c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"STORY-setup-002 creates apps/api from scratch (\"Create the canonical Express + TypeScript backend skeleton under apps/api\"), and STORY-setup-004 scaffolds apps/web via Angular CLI. In other words, the later stories create apps/api and apps/web themselves — we shouldn't create those placeholder packages (risk of conflict).\n\nSo then, where do the sample unit tests for this story go? The root itself is a package (root package.json) but not a workspace member. Options:\n\nOption C (revisited): the root's \"test\" script needs to be `turbo run test` per the acceptance criteria. But we can make the sample unit tests run via `turbo run test`… no, that's not possible. Turbo only runs tasks inside workspace packages.\n\nHmm wait — can turbo run tasks in the root package? No. Turbo runs in workspace packages. The root package.json is not a workspace member (workspaces: [\"apps/*\"]).\n\nOption D: put the sample tests at the root, and have the root's test script be `turbo run test` (per the criteria)… then when the user runs `npm test`, turbo runs tests in each app. Initially no apps exist → turbo warns \"No packages found\" and exits 0. The sample unit tests at the root won't run via the scripts. But the tests exist and pass if you run them directly. The gate says \"sample passing unit tests only\" — the requirement is that sample tests exist and pass, and that the story's acceptance criteria are verified. The acceptance criteria of this story are about the scaffold itself. A root-level test file that verifies the scaffold (package.json fields, turbo.json tasks, file existence, turbo version) is literally \"unit tests that map 1:1 to the acceptance criteria.\"\n\nTo make those executable and verified, the implementer can add a sample test that gets run during verification with `node --test test/`. And to route it through the root `npm test`… the root's test script must be `turbo run test` per the criteria. But wait — rereading the criteria: \"the scripts define build, test, and lint as turbo run build / turbo run test / turbo run lint.\" So root scripts.test = \"turbo run test\" is mandatory. OK.\n\nSo: root-level `test/scaffold.test.mjs` (or `test/monorepo.test.mjs`) using node:test + node:assert, run with `node --test test/`. The implementer runs this as verification (and it passes). This satisfies \"sample passing unit tests.\" Later when apps/api and apps/web exist, `turbo run test` will run their test scripts.\n\nHmm, but is it acceptable for `npm test` to run nothing in the initial state? It exits 0 (turbo warns but exits 0 when there are no packages). Wait, let me actually double-check: turbo 2.x `turbo run build` with an empty workspace — does it exit 0 or exit 1? I seem to recall turbo 2 outputs \"• Packages in scope: \" and \"No packages found in workspace\" and exits 0. Yes, I'm fairly confident it's exit 0 (it's a warning, not an error). The implementer will verify anyway (they'll run all the acceptance criteria checks including `npx turbo --version`).\n\nAlso — should we create the `apps/` directory itself? A git repo can't track empty directories. npm workspaces work fine with a glob that doesn't match anything. But it's nice to have the directory exist with a `.gitkeep`? Hmm, a `.gitkeep` inside apps/ is harmless and makes the structure explicit. Later stories will populate it. I'll have the implementer create `apps/.gitkeep` — no wait, could it interfere with `apps/*` glob matching? npm only matches directories with a package.json, so no. Turbo ignores it too. Safe.\n\nNow, what turbo.json to use in turbo 2.x? Turbo 2.x turbo.json:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://turborepo.com/schema.json\",\n  \"tasks\": {\n    \"build\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"dist/**\"]\n    },\n    \"test\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^test\"]  // hmm, or \"^build\"?\n    },\n    \"lint\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^lint\"]  // or depends on build?\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNote: in Turbo 2.0, the \"pipeline\" key was renamed to \"tasks\". The criteria say \"turbo.json defines the build, test, lint tasks with correct dependsOn and output caching for dist artifacts.\" So:\n- build: dependsOn [\"^build\"], outputs [\"dist/**\"] (caching for dist artifacts)\n- test: dependsOn [\"^build\"] or [\"^test\"] — standard is: tests depend on ^build (build upstream deps before testing), or ^test. Common Turborepo convention: `\"test\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^test\"] }` hmm. The Turborepo docs examples use `\"lint\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^lint\"] }` and `\"test\": { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] }`… actually the docs example is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tasks\": {\n    \"build\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"dist/**\"]\n    },\n    \"lint\": {},\n    \"test\": {\n      \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me not overthink this: reasonable choices:\n- build: { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"], \"outputs\": [\"dist/**\"] }\n- test: { \"dependsOn\": [\"^build\"] } — ensures upstream packages are built before tests.\n- lint: { \"dependsOn\": [\"^lint\"] } — lint propagates, or {} — lint has no dependencies.\n\nThe criteria just say \"correct dependsOn and output caching for dist artifacts.\" The implementer/planner can decide. I'll let the planner propose and the implementer implement, and the reviewer will verify against the criteria. I'll give the implementer guidance: turbo 2.x uses the \"tasks\" key (not \"pipeline\"), and the build outputs are dist/**.\n\nAlso, should we add a global turbo environment? Keep it minimal — no env vars yet. Keep it simple. Maybe add \"globalDependencies\": [\"**/.env*\"]? Not required. Minimal.\n\npackageManager: \"npm@10\" — hmm, one concern: does turbo read the `packageManager` field? Turbo 2 respects packageManager for running package manager commands. \"npm@10\" — does turbo parse it? If corepack isn't enabled, turbo will just run `npm` via shell. I don't think turbo validates the format. And `npx turbo --version` doesn't care. npm itself ignores the packageManager field (it's a corepack convention). Node 22.23.2 ships with npm 10.9.8. No problem.\n\nengines: { \"node\": \"22.x\" } — npm install checks engines only with the engine-strict setting (default false) → just a warning. The current node is 22.23.2 so no problem.\n\nNow, about pinning the turbo version: \"devDependencies pins turbo 2.x.\" Latest turbo 2.x on npm — the implementer should run `npm view turbo dist-tags.latest` and pick the latest 2.x. As of August 2026, turbo is probably 2.5.x. The implementer can query the registry. Pin strictly (exact version, no caret) to honor \"pin\"? Or ^2.x? \"Pins turbo 2.x\" — I'll interpret it as: devDependencies.turbo is a 2.x version, and to be safe use an exact pin (e.g., \"2.5.9\") — an exact pin is unambiguously a \"pin.\" Hmm, but if the registry's latest 2.x is X, then exact pin = X. Test assertion: semver-major(turbo) == 2. I'll let the implementer decide: pin to the exact latest 2.x found via `npm view turbo dist-tags.latest`.\n\nWait, one more thing: network. The registry is npmjs.org. The implementer will run npm install — needs network. The ralph environment probably has network (it's a CI-like orchestrator). I'll quickly verify with `npm ping --registry` (timeout 15s) now so we know early. Actually the implementer will find out. But if offline, the whole plan changes (local cache?). Let me just do a quick `npm ping` right now — it's cheap.\n\nAlso the `designs/` directory exists with HTML prototypes — irrelevant to this scaffolding story (no UI yet).\n\nSkill loading: \"the implementation subagent must load tdd-workflow before coding.\" Is there a tdd-workflow under .claude/skills/ecc/? The list was cut off at cpp-testing (head -40). Let me verify whether tdd-workflow exists. Also I'll check the skills that will be relevant: `tdd-workflow`, and probably nothing else needed for the root scaffold. The implementer needs to know the TDD approach — for a config-only story: write sample unit tests first (a scaffold test that fails because the files don't exist yet), then create the files until the tests pass. That's genuine TDD for this story.\n\nSo let me verify now: ls .claude/skills/ecc/ | grep -i tdd, npm ping.\n","signature":"4a0097f562824281a311a811bc623a4b"}],"model":"unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":53232,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8aacf81-83d2-40bc-b57b-a48cd1e47c7b","uuid":"70b5b270-7657-4086-ab5c-5d755c9004eb","timestamp":"2026-08-20T07:28:46.770Z"}
